      "problem": "Solve the equation $6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3z.$ We can factor out the $3z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z & =0 \\\\ 3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{z}^{2}-7z-30$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0 \\\\ 3z(2z+5)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=-\\frac{5}{2},$ and $z=6.$",
